<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>Hearing Dogs for Deaf People match trained dogs with people who need them. After merging with Hearing Link to expand their emotional support services, the team suspected there were serious gaps, especially around long wait times, end-of-life care for dogs and whether their new digital tools were actually working. They needed someone to find out.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="hd-what">
  <h2 id="hd-what">What I did</h2>
  <p>I designed the research approach and led 28 in-depth interviews with d/Deaf and hard of hearing people at every stage of the journey: those waiting for a dog, those living with one and those who had experienced retirement or loss. Each session combined qualitative exploration with light usability testing of a new digital support prototype.</p>
  <p>Accessible research wasn't an add-on. Sessions ran via participants' preferred platforms, with closed captions, proper lighting for lipreading, BSL interpreters where needed and any other accommodations requested. Making sure people could participate comfortably and confidently was a core part of the design.</p>
  <p>I coded and synthesised all transcripts, then worked alongside in-house service designers to translate findings into recommendations that shaped both products and services.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="hd-found">
  <h2 id="hd-found">What we found</h2>
  <p>The waiting period was the first thing people described, and it was brutal. Years could pass with no updates, no check-ins, nothing. Most people assumed they'd been forgotten. Several weren't even sure they were still on the list.</p>
  <p>When dogs eventually retired or died, the support largely disappeared too. People described real grief, a sudden loss of purpose and very little from the organisation to acknowledge it. Some were given contact details for Hearing Link but had no idea what it was or whether it was meant for them.</p>
  <p>That confusion around the Hearing Link merger ran throughout the research. Most participants hadn't heard of it. Others weren't sure if it was a separate charity or what it offered. The emotional support it provided was invisible to the people who needed it most.</p>
  <p>The digital prototype added friction rather than reducing it. Login barriers and cluttered interfaces meant several participants couldn't use it at all. Those who could said the content felt generic, advice they'd already seen elsewhere that didn't reflect their actual situation.</p>
  <p>Perhaps most quietly, the research surfaced how overlooked people with acquired hearing loss often felt. The support model skewed toward culturally Deaf sign language users. People who had lost hearing gradually, or later in life, often didn't know whether they were "deaf enough" to deserve help.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="hd-changed">
  <h2 id="hd-changed">What changed</h2>
  <p>The research gave the Hearing Dogs team a much clearer picture of the lived reality of their community, including quiet needs that had never shown up in formal feedback channels. It reshaped how and when they introduce people to Hearing Link, how they communicate with people during the wait and how they approach loss and post-retirement support as a core part of the journey rather than an afterthought. The digital tool was refined based on real access and usability challenges.</p>
</section>
